+{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdMZ1RzPiTvKo9p3oSgGU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016MCw15XqaLoeyeVzge7sGf","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/README.md","content":"# Reactive Spreadsheet Engine\n\nA spreadsheet engine as a TypeScript library: a grid of cells holding values or\nformulas, where changing a cell automatically recomputes everything that depends\non it.\n\n```ts\nimport { Spreadsheet } from './src/index.js';\n\nconst sheet = new Spreadsheet();\n\nsheet.set('A1', 10);\nsheet.set('A2', 20);\nsheet.set('A3', '=A1+A2');\nsheet.get('A3');        // 30\nsheet.getDisplay('A3'); // \"30\"\n\nsheet.set('A1', 50);\nsheet.get('A3');        // 70 — recomputed automatically\n\nsheet.set('B1', '=SUM(A1:A2)');       // 70\nsheet.set('C1', '=IF(A1>30, \"big\", \"small\")');  // \"big\"\n```\n\n## API\n\n| Method | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| `set(cell, value)` | Stores a number, string, boolean, or a formula (a string starting with `=`). Setting `''` clears the cell. |\n| `get(cell)` | Computed value, or `null` for an empty cell. Errors come back as their code, e.g. `'#DIV/0!'`. |\n| `getDisplay(cell)` | Display string for the computed value; `''` for an empty cell. |\n| `getCells()` | `Map` of every non-empty cell to its computed value, in reading order. A snapshot, not a live view. |\n| `getRaw(cell)` | What was passed to `set` — the formula source for formula cells. |\n| `clear(cell)` / `clearAll()` | Empty one cell, or the whole sheet. |\n\n## Formula language\n\n- **Operators** `+ - * /`, `^` for exponentiation, `&` for text concatenation,\n  parentheses for grouping, and unary `+`/`-`.\n- **Comparisons** `= <> > < >= <=`, which bind more loosely than arithmetic, so\n  `=1+1=2` is `true`.\n- **References** `A1`, `Z100`, `AA1`, up to `XFD1048576`. Case-insensitive, and\n  `$A$1` is accepted as a synonym for `A1`.\n- **Ranges** `A1:A5` (column), `A1:C1` (row), `A1:C3` (block). Corners may be\n  given in either order.\n- **Functions** `SUM`, `AVERAGE`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `COUNT`, `IF`. Names are\n  case-insensitive; the aggregates take any mix of ranges, references, and\n  values.\n\n## Behaviour worth knowing\n\n- **Reactivity** is eager. A `set` recomputes the cell and everything downstream\n  in dependency order, so no formula ever reads a stale intermediate value.\n- **Ranges track cells that do not exist yet.** `=SUM(A1:A5)` updates when `A3`\n  is first written, and again when it is cleared.\n- **Circular references throw.** `set` raises `CircularReferenceError` — which\n  carries the offending `cycle` — and leaves the sheet exactly as it was. Cycles\n  through any number of intermediate cells, or through a range, are caught.\n- **Other failures are values, not exceptions**, so one bad cell cannot break the\n  sheet: `#DIV/0!`, `#VALUE!`, `#NAME?`, `#NUM!`, and `#ERROR!` (a formula that\n  does not parse). Errors propagate to dependent cells.\n- **Coercion follows spreadsheet convention.** Empty cells count as `0`, booleans\n  as `1`/`0`, and numeric text is parsed. Aggregates skip text and booleans found\n  in the sheet but coerce values written directly into the formula. Comparisons\n  order numbers before text before booleans.\n- **`IF` is lazy** — only the branch it takes is evaluated, so\n  `=IF(A1=0, 0, 1/A1)` is safe.\n- **Display trims floating point noise.** `0.1 + 0.2` stores\n  `0.30000000000000004` and displays `0.3`.\n\n## Development\n\n```sh\nnpm test          # vitest\nnpm run lint      # eslint\nnpm run build     # tsc -> dist/\n```\n\n## Design notes\n\nEach cell records the cells and ranges its formula reads, and the engine keeps\nthe reverse edges so a change can find its dependents directly. Ranges stay\nunexpanded — `SUM(A1:A1000)` is one edge, not a thousand — which is what lets a\nrange react to cells created inside it later.\n\nRecomputation is a depth-first search over dependents; the reverse of its\npostorder is a topological order of exactly the affected cells. Cycle detection\nsearches upstream and downstream in lockstep and stops with the smaller side, so\nappending to a long chain stays cheap instead of rescanning the chain each time.\nBoth searches are iterative, so chains of 10,000+ cells cannot overflow the\nstack.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2597,"cache_read_input_tokens":112444,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2597},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ea6012bb-2013-407f-ae38-85a28445de44","uuid":"56bad893-2cc9-4331-a5b2-29d2374a56fb","request_id":"req_011CdMZ1Q25R1divuiyPnqwF"}
